WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.707
This slide is an optional preview of a later SQL topic.

00:00:03.707 --> 00:00:09.322
The function LEAD is a window function, and window functions are covered later in the course.

00:00:09.322 --> 00:00:12.167
You do not need to memorize LEAD here.

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:18.232
The reason this example appears in Module 6 is that the same grain reasoning still matters.

00:00:18.232 --> 00:00:23.037
Inside the common table expression, one row represents one admission,

00:00:23.037 --> 00:00:26.817
along with information about the next admission for the same patient.

00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:30.992
LEAD looks forward within each patient's admissions ordered by date.

00:00:30.992 --> 00:00:34.672
It retrieves the next department and the next admission date.

00:00:34.672 --> 00:00:39.972
The outer query keeps cases where a later admission exists and the department changes.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:42.182
Then it groups those transitions.

00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:49.077
In the current practice data, one example of a department change is a patient moving from Endocrinology to

00:00:49.077 --> 00:00:52.187
Cardiology on a later admission.

00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:57.567
After grouping, one row represents one transition pattern, such as one department to another department.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:02.662
Again, the important point for this module is not the window-function syntax.

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:08.407
The important point is to keep asking what one row represents after each transformation.

00:01:08.407 --> 00:01:11.707
We will study window functions in more detail later.
